{"packet_id":"17f5f269c325687f","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether public evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: a consumer proposition, a professional-line extension, no expansion, and a route-to-market change. It also requires value capture for Tennant. It does not reach rung 3. It flags no missed option or false premise. It also calls consumer reach unknown without examining the 'Sales direct to consumer' line in the 10-Q source, which the agent later retrieved (A4). The €20,000 / 12-week budget is an assumption, not an observed fact."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The better theory, T1 (A9), names a benefit: premium margins from a lifestyle or status product. Its behaviour chain is not grounded. It assumes households will buy multiple floor-cleaning units because they are status symbols. It also says value is captured by 'leveraging its existing distribution network', but no source shows Tennant has retail consumer reach. T2 (A10) is internally incoherent: it claims consumers will 'accept lower prices' and pay less because items are essential, and will also buy multiple units."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 and T2 are near-templated copies. Both have the same structure, the same retail-plus-subscription channel and the same 'multiple units per household' mechanism. Their third predictions are word-for-word identical. They differ only in framing (status versus health) and price direction, not in product, customer or channel."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"Some predictions are nominally observable, such as units per household and survey willingness to pay. They are vague, and they would not put the theories in trouble because they share the same